        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains November 7 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, such as 2 of 5.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For November 7 in music history, the Hot 100 chart week that includes November 7, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"You Light Up My Life" had the longest run, with ten weeks starting October 15, 1977.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and "Midnight Train to Georgia" was in the last week of its two.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the November 7 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called November 7.</p>
        <p>This page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains November 7 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>

        <h2 id="early">November 7 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Family Act, a Singer and a Songwriter's Hit</h2>
        <p>The first three Novembers of the decade were led by a hit group, a solo star and a songwriter-singer.</p>

        <h3>1970: "I'll Be There" by the Jackson 5</h3>
        <p>"I'll Be There" was the Jackson 5's fourth number one in a row, after "I Want You Back," "ABC" and "The Love You Save."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia credits Berry Gordy, Hal Davis, Bob West and Willie Hutch as its writers.</p>
        <p>It ran five weeks at number one, from October 17 to November 14, and November 7 was the fourth.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/ill-be-there-jackson-5/">I'll Be There Jackson 5</a> page.</p>
        <p>More of their hits are collected on <a href="/blog/artists/jackson-5-songs/">Jackson 5 Songs</a>.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Gypsys, Tramps & Thieves" by Cher</h3>
        <p>Wikipedia calls "Gypsys, Tramps & Thieves" Cher's first solo number one in Canada and the United States.</p>
        <p>Bob Stone wrote it, and Snuff Garrett produced it.</p>
        <p>The RIAA certified the single gold for a million copies shipped in the United States.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks at number one, and the week of November 6 was the first.</p>

        <h3>1972: "I Can See Clearly Now" by Johnny Nash</h3>
        <p>Johnny Nash produced "I Can See Clearly Now" himself.</p>
        <p>He recorded it in London with members of the Fabulous Five Inc.</p>
        <p>Ray Charles covered the song on his 1977 album True to Life.</p>
        <p>It topped the chart for four weeks, and November 4 was the first.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">November 7 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Grammy Winner, a Jackson 5 Cameo and an Elton John Return</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a Grammy winner, a Stevie Wonder single and an Elton John return.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Midnight Train to Georgia" by Gladys Knight & the Pips</h3>
        <p>Jim Weatherly wrote "Midnight Train to Georgia," and he first recorded it as "Midnight Plane to Houston."</p>
        <p>It was the group's first Hot 100 number one, according to Wikipedia.</p>
        <p>It won the 1974 Grammy for Best R&B Vocal Performance by a Duo, Group or Chorus.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks on top, and November 3 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The whole story is on <a href="/blog/songs/midnight-train-to-georgia/">Midnight Train to Georgia</a>.</p>
        <p>More of their records are on <a href="/blog/artists/gladys-knight-songs/">Gladys Knight Songs</a>.</p>

        <h3>1974: "You Haven't Done Nothin'" by Stevie Wonder</h3>
        <p>"You Haven't Done Nothin'" came from Stevie Wonder's album Fulfillingness' First Finale.</p>
        <p>The Jackson 5 sing background vocals on it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia notes the single was released on August 7, 1974, one day before Nixon announced his resignation.</p>
        <p>It held the top for one week, starting November 2.</p>
        <p>More of his records are on <a href="/blog/artists/stevie-wonder/">Stevie Wonder</a>.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Island Girl" by Elton John</h3>
        <p>"Island Girl" was the first single from the Elton John album Rock of the Westies.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one for three weeks on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>The same source says John has not performed it since 1990, with Rolling Stone's Andy Greene pointing to the lyrics as a likely reason.</p>
        <p>November 1 was the first of its three weeks.</p>
        <p>If you like Elton John, <a href="/blog/artists/elton-john/">Elton John</a> has his own page here.</p>

        <h2 id="late">November 7 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Rock, a Record, a Ballad and a Synth-Pop Surprise</h2>
        <p>The last four Novembers brought the decade's longest run and a one-week hit from a new sound.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Rock'n Me" by the Steve Miller Band</h3>
        <p>"Rock'n Me" was the second single from Fly Like an Eagle, the band's ninth studio album.</p>
        <p>Steve Miller said the song's intro was a nod to the English rock band Free.</p>
        <p>It held number one for a single week, starting November 6, 1976.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Light Up My Life" by Debby Boone</h3>
        <p>"You Light Up My Life" ran ten weeks at number one, from October 15 to December 17, 1977.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says that set a new Hot 100 record for most weeks at number one.</p>
        <p>Joseph Brooks wrote it, and Kasey Cisyk first recorded it for the film's soundtrack.</p>
        <p>November 5 was the fourth of the ten weeks.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "You Needed Me" by Anne Murray</h3>
        <p>Randy Goodrum wrote "You Needed Me," and he described it as being about unconditional undeserved love.</p>
        <p>Anne Murray won the Grammy for Best Female Pop Vocal Performance, the first Grammy awarded to a Canadian artist.</p>
        <p>It held the top for one week, starting November 4.</p>
        <p>The song is number 63 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Pop Muzik" by M</h3>
        <p>Robin Scott wrote and produced "Pop Muzik" and sang lead as the band M.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100 and also topped the Australian ARIA singles chart.</p>
        <p>It spent one week at number one, starting November 3.</p>

